1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:07,063
This video walks you through running a usability test with a real participant, from the moment they arrive to the last task.

2
00:00:07,667 --> 00:00:13,320
It follows the UX Testing Guide, so you can watch it now and keep the guide open during your own sessions.

3
00:00:14,383 --> 00:00:20,084
A usability session has a simple shape. First, you welcome the participant and explain why you're there.

4
00:00:20,084 --> 00:00:23,045
You're improving the design. You are not testing them.

5
00:00:23,633 --> 00:00:26,092
Next, confirm consent and confidentiality.

6
00:00:26,092 --> 00:00:30,951
Be open about recording and observers, and make it clear they can stop at any time.

7
00:00:31,567 --> 00:00:35,236
Then reassure them. We are not testing you. We are testing the application.

8
00:00:35,833 --> 00:00:42,212
Give tasks one at a time, as realistic scenarios with no hints, and ask them to think aloud while they work.

9
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:48,219
Finally, thank them, ask for their overall impressions, and remind them how their data will be used.

10
00:00:49,283 --> 00:00:52,995
Start by introducing yourself and setting a friendly, relaxed tone.

11
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:59,595
Watch your wording. Call it a study, or research, rather than a test. The word test can make people nervous.

12
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:05,939
Your welcome has a few jobs. Help the participant feel comfortable, and explain what will happen and why.

13
00:01:06,533 --> 00:01:12,976
Remind them that taking part is voluntary and confidential, and get them ready for the tasks and for thinking aloud.

14
00:01:14,017 --> 00:01:19,652
The guide lists seven points to cover. Begin with a greeting and a thank you. Then explain the purpose.

15
00:01:19,652 --> 00:01:25,451
You're testing the design to see how well it supports real tasks, and there are no right or wrong answers.

16
00:01:26,067 --> 00:01:31,313
Give a quick overview: a few tasks based on real-world scenarios, thinking aloud as they go.

17
00:01:31,313 --> 00:01:35,304
Mention the recording and any observers, who won't interact with them.

18
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:41,329
Tell them their responses stay confidential, and they can stop or skip a question at any time.

19
00:01:41,329 --> 00:01:47,335
Ask for honest feedback. If something is confusing or frustrating, that's exactly what you need to know.

20
00:01:47,933 --> 00:01:51,176
Last of all, ask if they have any questions before you begin.

21
00:01:52,217 --> 00:01:57,033
Be open about observers and recording, and confirm it again at the start of the session.

22
00:01:57,033 --> 00:02:02,178
You might say: this session will be recorded so we can review it later and improve the design.

23
00:02:02,178 --> 00:02:08,473
It won't be shared outside the team, and the observers are here to learn from your experience, not to evaluate you.

24
00:02:09,067 --> 00:02:15,019
Why does this matter? Transparency builds trust. People need to know who is watching and what's being recorded.

25
00:02:15,633 --> 00:02:17,548
There's an ethical side too.

26
00:02:17,548 --> 00:02:23,633
Many organisations follow GDPR or similar privacy rules, so explicit consent is required.

27
00:02:24,233 --> 00:02:29,567
And if participants feel blindsided by observers or recording, they may act unnaturally.

28
00:02:30,617 --> 00:02:39,382
Before the session, your consent form should say clearly whether you'll record audio, video or the screen, who can access the recordings, and what they're for.

29
00:02:39,382 --> 00:02:41,091
Get written or digital consent.

30
00:02:41,700 --> 00:02:48,098
At the start, reconfirm it out loud. Just to remind you, this session will be recorded for research purposes.

31
00:02:48,098 --> 00:02:51,151
Then give them a chance to ask questions or opt out.

32
00:02:51,767 --> 00:02:53,611
Explain what the observers are for.

33
00:02:53,611 --> 00:02:58,935
They're there to learn from the participant's experience, not to judge them, and they'll stay silent.

34
00:02:59,533 --> 00:03:01,002
And give them some control.

35
00:03:01,002 --> 00:03:06,168
If they feel uncomfortable, they can pause or stop the recording, or skip any question or task.

36
00:03:07,217 --> 00:03:10,291
Next, ask the participant to sign a consent form.

37
00:03:10,291 --> 00:03:14,557
Send a link if the session is remote, or use a paper form in person.

38
00:03:14,557 --> 00:03:20,267
Informed consent means they fully understand what the study involves and agree voluntarily.

39
00:03:20,267 --> 00:03:25,286
It's an ethical requirement, and often a legal one, under GDPR in the UK and EU.

40
00:03:25,900 --> 00:03:34,668
A good consent form covers the purpose of the study, what will happen, and how data is handled: what you'll collect, how it's stored, who can access it, and for how long.

41
00:03:35,267 --> 00:03:45,528
It also makes clear that taking part is voluntary, sets out any risks, such as the discomfort of being recorded, explains how you'll keep them anonymous, and gives a contact for questions.

42
00:03:46,583 --> 00:03:51,917
In practice, give them the written form before the session, in plain language without legal jargon.

43
00:03:52,533 --> 00:03:56,331
Then reconfirm verbally when you begin, and leave time for questions.

44
00:03:57,383 --> 00:04:03,399
One sentence does a lot of work in a usability test. We are not testing you. We are testing the application.

45
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,332
It reduces anxiety. Participants who feel judged try to do well instead of behaving naturally.

46
00:04:09,332 --> 00:04:13,472
Hearing this helps them relax and use the product as they normally would.

47
00:04:14,067 --> 00:04:19,669
It encourages honest feedback. People who feel tested may avoid admitting confusion or mistakes.

48
00:04:19,669 --> 00:04:25,331
Once they know the product is being evaluated, they're more likely to share genuine frustrations.

49
00:04:25,933 --> 00:04:27,521
It keeps the session neutral.

50
00:04:27,521 --> 00:04:34,531
You're watching how the design supports the task, not how skilled the user is, so any problems are likely down to the interface.

51
00:04:35,133 --> 00:04:39,094
And it improves your data. Stress and fear of judgement skew results.

52
00:04:39,094 --> 00:04:44,776
A relaxed participant gives you more authentic insights, and that leads to better design decisions.

53
00:04:45,817 --> 00:04:48,191
Now for the tasks. Give them one at a time.

54
00:04:48,191 --> 00:04:56,697
That keeps the participant focused, stops them changing their behaviour because they know what's coming, and captures a natural reaction to each scenario.

55
00:04:57,300 --> 00:05:01,409
Frame each task as a real-world goal, not an instruction like click here.

56
00:05:01,409 --> 00:05:06,644
For example: imagine you want to book a train ticket for tomorrow. Show me how you'd do that.

57
00:05:07,233 --> 00:05:10,782
Give context, but no hints. Don't tell them which feature to use.

58
00:05:10,782 --> 00:05:13,676
When they've finished a task, introduce the next one.

59
00:05:14,717 --> 00:05:19,559
Ask participants to think aloud. It shows you their thought process, not just their actions.

60
00:05:20,167 --> 00:05:25,375
At the start, say something like: as you go through the tasks, please say out loud what you're thinking.

61
00:05:25,375 --> 00:05:29,532
What you expect to happen, what you're looking for, and anything that confuses you.

62
00:05:30,133 --> 00:05:35,232
During the tasks, a gentle nudge helps. Keep talking as you go. What's going through your mind?

63
00:05:35,833 --> 00:05:40,548
If they go quiet, ask: what are you thinking right now? Or: what made you choose that option?

64
00:05:41,583 --> 00:05:45,957
A few tips for facilitators. Don't lead the participant, and don't correct them.

65
00:05:46,567 --> 00:05:52,199
Stick to neutral prompts, such as: tell me what you're looking for. Or: what do you expect to happen next?

66
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:57,003
And avoid phrases that sound like judgement, such as: why did you do that?

67
00:05:58,050 --> 00:06:01,591
To finish, here are some quick prompts to keep beside you during a session.

68
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:05,690
Welcome them and remind them you're testing the design, not them.

69
00:06:05,690 --> 00:06:10,093
Tell them the session is recorded, and that they can stop or withdraw at any time.

70
00:06:10,700 --> 00:06:15,585
Keep them talking with: what are you thinking right now? And give them one scenario at a time.

71
00:06:16,200 --> 00:06:26,376
At the end, thank them for their time, ask for their overall impressions, remind them their responses are confidential, and give them your contact details for any questions later.

72
00:06:27,417 --> 00:06:31,982
That's the whole session. Keep the guide open when you run your own tests, and good luck.
